<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">K'inich Yax K'uk' Mo'</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 426–437</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">K'inich Yax K'uk' Mo' was the founder of the royal dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copan">Copán</a>, in present-day Honduras, who took power in 426 CE. Inscriptions link him to Teotihuacan and Tikal, and a tomb believed to be his lies deep beneath the Acropolis. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1715">Fifteen</a> later kings traced their descent from him, and his agents refounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quirigua">Quiriguá</a> as a dependency of Copán.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kinich-yax-kuk-mo.jpg" alt="K'inich Yax K'uk' Mo'" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DuendeThumb · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yax_Kuk_Mo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Maya cities, links to Teotihuacan became a source of prestige: <b>K’inich Yax K’uk’ Mo’</b>, who founded the royal dynasty of Copán in 426, was later portrayed with Teotihuacan-style goggles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Copán valley had been farmed for centuries when, in 426 CE, <b>K'inich Yax K'uk' Mo'</b>, a lord with ties to Teotihuacan and Tikal, founded a dynasty that would rule for some four centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/copan">Copán</a></li><li><blockquote>Quiriguá was founded or refounded in 426 CE as a dependency of Copán, about 50 kilometres to the south, by agents of Copán's founder <b>K'inich Yax K'uk' Mo'</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/quirigua">Quiriguá</a></li></ul></section>
